WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
Everything we do today fits into three stages.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.920
Stage one. Transport.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.180
ATP-citrate lyase cleaves citrate to deliver acetyl CoA into the cytoplasm.

4
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.200
Stage two. Activation.

5
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.780
The committed step.

6
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:22.500
Acetyl CoA carboxylase spends an ATP to make malonyl CoA, locking the cell into

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.400
building fat. Stage three.

8
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:30.380
Elongation. Fatty acid synthase runs a four-step cycle over and over, adding two

9
00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.740
carbons each pass until it reaches palmitate.

10
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.880
Notice the logic: get the bricks to the site, commit irreversibly, then build.

11
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.320
If you remember nothing else, remember transport, activation, elongation, in that

12
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.320
order.

13
00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:47.173
Here is the decision point of the entire pathway.

14
00:00:47.973 --> 00:00:54.413
Acetyl CoA carboxylase takes acetyl CoA, bicarbonate, and ATP and produces malonyl

15
00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:59.173
CoA. This is the committed step, the reaction that sends these carbons into new

16
00:00:59.173 --> 00:01:05.353
fatty acids. The enzyme depends on biotin, a vitamin cofactor whose job is to carry

17
00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:10.853
carbon dioxide. Think of biotin as a swinging arm that grabs a carboxyl group and

18
00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:16.253
delivers it. Because this step is committed and irreversible, it is exactly where

19
00:01:16.253 --> 00:01:17.493
regulation should sit.

20
00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:22.233
And indeed, this enzyme is the master control valve we will return to.

21
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:28.157
Now we build. Fatty acid synthase is one enzyme complex that runs a repeating

22
00:01:28.157 --> 00:01:32.697
four-step cycle, condensation, reduction, dehydration, reduction.

23
00:01:33.497 --> 00:01:34.857
Remember it as CRDR.

24
00:01:34.857 --> 00:01:39.257
Each turn of the cycle joins the growing chain to a fresh two-carbon unit and then

25
00:01:39.257 --> 00:01:42.017
chemically grooms the new linkage into a saturated stretch.

26
00:01:42.737 --> 00:01:48.077
The cycle is not run once, but seven times, because each pass adds two carbons and

27
00:01:48.077 --> 00:01:50.857
we are aiming for a 16-carbon product, palmitate.

28
00:01:50.857 --> 00:01:56.057
The elegance is that a single enzyme assembly does the same four moves repeatedly,

29
00:01:56.677 --> 00:02:00.497
like a machine stamping out identical parts on an assembly line.

30
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.060
Here is the chemical trick that makes synthesis go.

31
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.460
We have a primer, the acetyl group, and an extender, the malonyl group.

32
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.720
When they condense, the malonyl loses its extra carboxyl as carbon dioxide.

33
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.300
That decarboxylation is the engine.

34
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.660
Releasing carbon dioxide is so thermodynamically favorable that it drags the

35
00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.280
otherwise sluggish condensation forward, the way letting go of a heavy weight pulls

36
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.300
the whole rope along.

37
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:33.700
The product, acetoacetyl-ACP, forms readily because the cell paid for that carbon

38
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.580
dioxide earlier with ATP.

39
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.020
So the carbon we added in stage two leaves here, but its purpose was always to power

40
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.020
this step.

41
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.440
Step back and ask a fair question.

42
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.500
Why spend an ATP to attach a carbon dioxide in stage two only to throw that same

43
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.800
carbon dioxide away during condensation?

44
00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.420
It looks wasteful.

45
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.580
The answer is that the cell is buying thermodynamics.

46
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.160
Direct condensation of two acetyl units is unfavorable and would barely proceed.

47
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.780
By investing one ATP to carboxylate first, the cell stores energy in malonyl CoA.

48
00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.320
Then, when that carbon dioxide is released, its departure makes condensation

49
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.360
strongly downhill.

50
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.120
It is a gambit. Pay up front, release on demand, and gain control over a reaction

51
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.440
that otherwise would not run.

52
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.200
Every carbon still traces back to acetyl CoA.
